Canadian Journal of Forest Research</source><year>2003</year><volume>33</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>783</fpage><lpage>795</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1139/x03-001</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Hille Ris Lambers J, Clark J (2003) Effects of dispersal, shrubs, and density-dependent mortality on tree seed and seedling distributions. Canadian Journal of Forest Research 33(5):783&#x02013;795 </citation></ref><ref id="CR13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hollingsworth</surname><given-names>RG</given-names></name><name><surname>Hain</surname><given-names>FP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Balsam woolly adelgid (Homoptera: Adelgidae) and spruce-fir decline in the southern Appalachians: assessing pest reliance in a damaged ecosystem</article-title><source>The Florida Entomologist</source><year>1991</year><volume>74</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>179</fpage><lpage>187</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2307/3495294</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Hollingsworth RG, Hain FP (1991) Balsam woolly adelgid (Homoptera: Adelgidae) and spruce-fir decline in the southern Appalachians: assessing pest reliance in a damaged ecosystem. The Florida Entomologist 74(2):179&#x02013;187 </citation></ref><ref id="CR14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Houston</surname><given-names>DR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Major new tree disease epidemics: Beech bark disease</article-title><source>Annual Review of Phytopathology</source><year>1994</year><volume>32</volume><fpage>75</fpage><lpage>87</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1146/annurev.py.32.090194.000451</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Houston DR (1994) Major new tree disease epidemics: Beech bark disease. Annual Review of Phytopathology 32:75&#x02013;87 </citation></ref><ref id="CR15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jenkins</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name><name><surname>Aber</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Canham</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Hemlock woolly adelgid impacts on community structure and N cycling rates in eastern hemlock forests</article-title><source>Canadian Journal of Forest Research</source><year>1999</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>630</fpage><lpage>645</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1139/cjfr-29-5-630</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Jenkins JC, Aber JD, Canham CD (1999) Hemlock woolly adelgid impacts on community structure and N cycling rates in eastern hemlock forests. Canadian Journal of Forest Research 19:630&#x02013;645 </citation></ref><ref id="CR16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jenkins</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Impact of the balsam woolly adelgid (Adelges piceae Ratz.) on an Abies fraseri dominated stand near the summit of Mout LeCont, Tennessee</article-title><source>Castanea</source><year>2003</year><volume>68</volume><fpage>109</fpage><lpage>118</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Jenkins MA (2003) Impact of the balsam woolly adelgid (Adelges piceae Ratz.) on an Abies fraseri dominated stand near the summit of Mout LeCont, Tennessee. Castanea 68:109&#x02013;118 </citation></ref><ref id="CR17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jenkins</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Vegetation communities of Great Smoky Mountains National Park</article-title><source>Southeastern Naturalist</source><year>2007</year><volume>1</volume><fpage>35</fpage><lpage>56</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1656/1528-7092(2007)6[